  "strategy": "I propose a fundamentally different approach that combines deterministic optimization methods with self-adaptive structured variation. After analyzing the problem space, we find that the previous strategies rely too heavily on local gradient information and fail to escape certain global minima. The key insight is to model the step function as a discrete mixture density optimized against autocorrelation constraints.\n\nThe algorithm will:\n1. Use indicator functions instead of continuous coordinates to represent the step-like nature of the problem\n2. Implement a simulated annealing phase over a structured binary lattice projection space\n3. Incorporate a resembling-yet-distinct gradient coil based on polynomial smoothing terms\n\nMathematical specification:\nThe optimization will occur over binary indicator vectors x_i \u2208 {0,1} projected onto a simplex that partitions [0,1000]. The objective is defined as:\nmin_{x} [ max_{k}(\u2211_{i+j=k} x_i x_j) ] / [ (1/\u2211 x_i) * (\u2211 x_i)^2 ]\n\nWe normalize the binary vector so that the total sum is sqrt(2*n), then compute the autocorrelation in full convolution mode. The constraints require non-negative components and undergo projection by a bitwise transformation.\n\nImplementation procedure steps:\n\nStep 1: Initialization\n- Start with all zeros, except set the first ceil($(n)/2$) components to 1\n- Edge case handling ensures the sum passes the normalized test\n\nStep 2: Gradient Surrogate Construction\n- Compute the continuous gradient as a bell-shaped curve\n- Defined as gradient_i = 1/(1+exp(-weighted_decay*(abs(x_i-0.5)/scale)))\n- This allows adaptation of the gradient distance from binary extremes\n\nStep 3: Objective Evaluation\n- Use the evaluate_sequence function as primary judge\n- The initial objective is computed from the binary parent indicator\n\nStep 4: Annealing Phase\n- Cook a schedule of deltas (including cos_phi and sin_theta terms) to vary the vector entropy\n- Lift properties by swapping indicators while maintaining simplex proportion\n- Offer patience rather than full stochastic perturbation since the solution space exists in polynomial-time accessible structures\n\nStep 5: Optimization Steps\n- Cycle over 1500 seconds with a primary minimization weight (deflates the secondary metric)\n- Secondary optimization weight adjusts the delta component contribution based on the lower envelope\n\nStep 6: Candidate Validation\n- Only transitions that improve the objective by at least 5% are accepted\n- Prefer positive feedback over standard simulated annealing acceptance (include 0 in the metric space)\n\nThis approach ensures that the structure of the step function is maintained by discrete components, while the gradient terms guide the search in a way that directly models the contribution of each unit to the autocorrelation peaks.",
